From 568309d2d092c8b790b18ebc4d9f55fc1cb322fd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: psigod <psigod@example.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 20:13:16 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 187/191] fs: Keep PID 1's previous fs_struct while its threads
 still use it

validate_fs_switch() points userspace_init_fs at nullfs as soon as PID 1
switches to another fs_struct. Android's init does that in its main thread
(unshare(CLONE_NEWNS) when it sets up its mount namespaces, on systems with
updatable APEXes) while its property service thread goes on running on the
old one. That thread then resolves every path from an empty root: opening
/sys/fs/selinux/access fails and every property set is refused ("SELinux
permission check failed", without an AVC), so the boot stops. Firmware and
usermode helper lookups, which also go through userspace_init_fs, would
fail the same way from then on.

The state is not stale while other threads of PID 1 still run on it, so
leave it alone then, as kernels did before nullfs.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 fs/fs_struct.c | 11 +++++++++++
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+)

diff --git a/fs/fs_struct.c b/fs/fs_struct.c
index 34699f3b6..047ab8525 100644
--- a/fs/fs_struct.c
+++ b/fs/fs_struct.c
@@ -192,6 +192,17 @@ static inline void validate_fs_switch(struct fs_struct *old_fs)
 	/* @old_fs may be dangling but for comparison it's fine */
 	if (old_fs != userspace_init_fs)
 		return;
+	/*
+	 * Not stale while other threads of PID 1 still run on it (the global
+	 * reference is the one left otherwise, and it keeps @old_fs alive):
+	 * Android's init unshares its mount namespace in the main thread while
+	 * its property service thread goes on resolving paths, for every
+	 * property set (/sys/fs/selinux/access). Leave it as older kernels did.
+	 */
+	if (READ_ONCE(old_fs->users) > 1) {
+		pr_info("VFS: Pid 1 stopped sharing filesystem state, its other threads still use it\n");
+		return;
+	}
 	pr_warn("VFS: Pid 1 stopped sharing filesystem state\n");
 	set_fs_root(userspace_init_fs, &init_fs.root);
 	set_fs_pwd(userspace_init_fs, &init_fs.root);
